      tag: "Stays at zero and MIXED, with the composition inside it changing rather than the level. The fiscal leg that was repaired yesterday held today rather than reversing: the OAT is 4.9220 against a 3.3550 Bund for a spread near 157 basis points, the BTP is 4.5100, and Newsquawk's read is that Moody's maintained Aa3 negative-outlook assessment has removed enough immediate pressure that a dovish ECB reassessment is unlikely. The leg that deteriorated is energy and it deteriorated quickly: EU gas is 96.44 euros per megawatt hour, up 10.32% on the week, 38.83% on the month and 242.48% year to date, which with September HICP at 3.8% and core 2.5% is the firmest inflation case on this board outside Australia and simultaneously a pure cost shock with no export offset. The cycle shows it - an October composite PMI of 51.9 at a four-month low with France contracting - against a Q2 GDP of 1.2% y/y that was the better surprise, unemployment stable at 6.4% and negotiated wage growth decelerating to 2.44%. Deposit rate 2.50%, refi 2.65%, and a decision in thirty-two hours.",
      drivers: "Deposit facility rate 2.50% and main refinancing rate 2.65% after the September 10 hike, with tomorrow's hold expected by 32 of 32 houses and 64 of 73 Reuters-polled economists expecting 25 basis points in December to 2.75%, September HICP 3.8% with core 2.5% and 0.6% m/m, Q2 GDP 1.2% y/y and 0.6% q/q, unemployment 6.4% with 11,357,000 unemployed, Q2 employment plus 0.1% and negotiated wage growth 2.44%, October composite PMI 51.9 at a four-month low, EU gas 96.44 euros and plus 10.32% on the week, OAT 4.9220 with a 157 basis point Bund spread, EUR/USD 1.11210 and EUR/CHF 0.92294.",
      forward: "\u{1F985} Hawkish but priced into tomorrow at 13:15 UTC, with the press conference at 13:45 carrying the whole event. Nobody expects a move and almost everybody expects December: 32 of 32 houses see the hold, 64 of 73 see 25 basis points in December to a 2.75% peak, and Deutsche Bank's baseline adds risk of a further hike to 3% in 2027. Energy is what makes that December hike hard to argue away - gas up 38.83% on the month feeds directly into a 3.8% HICP - so the hawkish path is close to fully subscribed and the euro's upside from it is limited. Two things could actually move it, and neither is the rate. Newsquawk expects France to dominate the Q&A, and whether Lagarde treats the French improvement as durable matters because Moody's own baseline assumes the budget eventually passes possibly by decree, while the National Assembly rejected the revenue portion of the social security bill on Friday - so a renewed no-confidence path puts the OAT back through 5% and the euro shorts straight back on. The second is whether she sets a departure date, with the WEF board meeting on November 2; commentary flags that as potentially more market-moving than the decision. Keep EUR/USD at RANGE and express the euro against the franc, where the other leg has nothing scheduled for six weeks."
