      tag: "Stays at minus 1 and WEAKENING, and it is now one of two currencies at the bottom rather than alone there - which matters mainly because it ends the NZD/CHF long. The structural case is unchanged and the SNB has published it itself: the policy rate is 0.00%, held on September 24, with nothing scheduled until December 10 and most analysts seeing no increase before 2028; the ten-year is 0.4570%, the lowest on this board and 476 basis points below Australia; September CPI is 1.0% with core 0.5% and the monthly rate flat. The summary of the Bank's September discussion, published October 22, attributed a roughly 3% trade-weighted decline since June to carry-trade demand, noted little safe-haven demand and judged heightened willingness to intervene no longer necessary while keeping the option to act - a central bank describing its own currency as a funding vehicle. IFR reports the franc displacing the yen in that role as BoJ hikes erode yen funding. The domestic economy is not weak - Q2 GDP 2.3% y/y, unemployment 3.0%, business confidence 109, a 55.3 manufacturing PMI - which is exactly why the rate can stay at zero.",
      drivers: "SNB policy rate 0.00% held on September 24 with nothing scheduled until December 10 and most analysts seeing no increase before 2028, September CPI 1.0% with core 0.5% and 0.0% m/m, unemployment 3.0% with 140,480 unemployed, Q2 GDP 2.3% y/y and 1.5% q/q with employment plus 0.5%, September manufacturing PMI 55.3 and business confidence 109, ten-year 0.4570 and the lowest on this board at 476 basis points below Australia, the September policy discussion attributing a 3% trade-weighted decline to carry demand, USD/CHF 0.82987 with EUR/CHF 0.92294 and AUD/CHF 0.58374 and plus 0.73% on the week.",
      forward: "\u{1F53B} Dovish by construction into December 10, and the honest note today is that the tail against the short got fatter rather than thinner. Nothing about Swiss policy can change for six weeks, which is precisely what makes the franc the funding leg of every trade in this file: AUD/CHF on a 476 basis point spread, EUR/CHF, GBP/CHF, CAD/CHF and USD/CHF all rest on the other currency's catalyst doing the work while this one has none. The invalidation is not Swiss at all and it moved the wrong way today: a Hormuz disruption persistent enough to convert a supply shock into a demand shock would restore the classic haven hierarchy, bid the franc and gold together and invert most of these crosses - and Iran's navy has just claimed a mine strike on a tanker and declared the strait closed to uncoordinated transits. The tells are specific: gold losing 4,000, a rise in the Navy-led JMIC threat level, and a tanker loss large enough to stop the convoys. The second, softer limit is official: UniCredit warns that a rapid return toward or below March's EUR/CHF low of 0.8981 could prompt direct intervention, which sits 2.6% below a 0.92294 spot. Size the franc shorts for that tail rather than avoid them."
