  geopolitics: "The transmission chain is unchanged and both ends of it moved today, in opposite directions for the first time in a week. On escalation: Iran navy said on October 27 that an oil tanker caught fire after striking naval mines east of the Strait of Hormuz, that the vessel had switched off its tracking and entered a restricted zone, and that the strait remains unsafe and closed to uncoordinated transits; the IRGC separately claimed two violating tankers were set ablaze. No maritime authority has independently confirmed either account, which is the same evidentiary position as the October 17 claim. Public trackers have the strait closed on day 241. On de-escalation, and this is the new input: reporting on October 28 says Qatar has put a draft proposal to Tehran setting a seven-day timeline with daily confidence-building steps, with a Saudi-led delegation alongside Pakistan and Turkey travelling to Tehran and a Qatar-Iran-Russia meeting in Moscow committing to continue dialogue. Iran foreign minister says the offer to reopen the strait within seven days remains on the table, while Tehran has publicly rejected reports that a deal is close; one tracker reports Iran seven conditions, centred on ending the US blockade, as the principal obstacle. Treat it as a live two-way risk rather than a resolution. The prices say the premium is still being paid: WTI 102.837 and plus 2.94% for plus 5.85% on the week, Brent 115.335 and plus 1.28%, EU gas 98.33 euros and plus 12.47% on the week, German gas 101.99 and plus 4.59%, UK gas 245.00 pence and plus 13.84%. The FX read now has an official endorsement it did not have this morning. Warsh named energy and related prices as having moved higher again in recent weeks, and Macklem hiked four hours earlier on the argument that energy prices are higher and expected to remain higher for longer. Two of the three largest central banks on this board are now treating the shock as an inflation risk rather than a relative-price move, which turns it from a growth drag into a tightening input - hawkish for everyone, but asymmetrically so. It is a terms-of-trade gain for Canada, which is why USD/CAD is short and USOIL long and why those two must stay consistent. It is a pure cost shock with no export offset for the euro area, the United Kingdom and above all Japan, where it is the named reason the yen was marked down. The rerouting evidence still caps the upside rather than removing it - strait flows near 82% of pre-war levels, total Middle East flows above 24 million barrels a day against 23.3 million pre-war, 40% of non-Iranian Gulf crude moving by pipeline against 17% - so this is a premium trade priced off insurable risk, not a shortage trade. The single cleanest invalidation for the whole complex is a confirmed Qatari-brokered reopening: it sells crude, sells the franc, sells gold, buys the euro-area and Japanese growth legs, and takes the terms-of-trade support out from under the loonie all at once.",
